{
 "cells": [
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# 👤 Face Detection with OpenCV Haar Cascade\n",
    "\n",
    "Welcome! In this project, we are going to explore **Computer Vision** using **OpenCV** (Open Source Computer Vision Library). \n",
    "Our goal is to implement **Face Detection** (locating faces in an image) rather than Face Recognition (identifying who the face belongs to). \n",
    "\n",
    "We will load a pre-trained machine learning model called a **Haar Cascade classifier**, preprocess our test photo by converting it to grayscale, run the detector to locate faces, and draw bounding boxes around them! 🚀✨"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 1. ⚙️ Setup & Imports\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's import OpenCV, NumPy, Pillow, and Matplotlib. OpenCV handles the computer vision algorithms, Pillow handles image parsing, and Matplotlib lets us show our visual results inline!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import os\n",
    "import urllib.request\n",
    "import cv2\n",
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n",
    "from PIL import Image, ImageDraw\n",
    "\n",
    "print(\"✅ Setup completed successfully!\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 2. 📁 Load pre-trained Haar Cascade\n",
    "\n",
    "A Haar Cascade is an object detection algorithm trained to find specific features. We use `haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml` which contains pre-extracted patterns representing human faces (like contrast regions between the eyes, eyebrows, and nose). \n",
    "\n",
    "Let's download this XML file from OpenCV's official GitHub repository if it's not already in our directory."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "cascade_url = \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/opencv/opencv/master/data/haarcascades/haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml\"\n",
    "cascade_path = \"../dataset/haarcascade_frontalface_default.xml\"\n",
    "\n",
    "if not os.path.exists(cascade_path):\n",
    "    print(\"📥 Downloading Haar Cascade XML model...\")\n",
    "    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(cascade_path), exist_ok=True)\n",
    "    urllib.request.urlretrieve(cascade_url, cascade_path)\n",
    "    print(\"✅ Download complete!\")\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f\"📦 Haar Cascade model located at: {cascade_path}\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 3. 📷 Load Test Image\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's load the sample group photo we generated, convert it to a NumPy array, and display it. Pillow uses RGB channels, while OpenCV by default reads images in BGR order - so we must make sure we convert colors properly if interfacing with OpenCV display routines."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "img_path = \"../Images/sample_group.png\"\n",
    "\n",
    "# Open image with Pillow\n",
    "img = Image.open(img_path)\n",
    "if img.mode != \"RGB\":\n",
    "    img = img.convert(\"RGB\")\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show original image\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 8))\n",
    "plt.imshow(img)\n",
    "plt.axis('off')\n",
    "plt.title(\"Original Image\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 4. 🌗 Grayscale Preprocessing\n",
    "\n",
    "Haar Cascades work by scanning contrast differences (edges, lines, and textures) rather than color values. Converts to grayscale simplifies the pixels into a single intensity channel (from 0 to 255), speeding up the grid search calculation significantly!"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Convert PIL image to NumPy array\n",
    "img_np = np.array(img)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Convert to grayscale using OpenCV\n",
    "gray = cv2.cvtColor(img_np, cv2.COLOR_RGB2GRAY)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show grayscale image\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 8))\n",
    "plt.imshow(gray, cmap='gray')\n",
    "plt.axis('off')\n",
    "plt.title(\"Grayscale Preprocessed Image\")\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 5. 👥 Run Face Detection\n",
    "\n",
    "We load the cascade classifier and call `detectMultiScale`. We tune two key parameters:\n",
    "- `scaleFactor=1.10`: Specifies how much the image size is reduced at each scale (1.10 = 10% reduction per step).\n",
    "- `minNeighbors=5`: Higher values reduce false boxes by requiring multiple matching scanning boxes to agree on a face."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Load Cascade Classifier\n",
    "face_cascade = cv2.CascadeClassifier(cascade_path)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Run detection\n",
    "faces = face_cascade.detectMultiScale(gray, scaleFactor=1.10, minNeighbors=5, minSize=(30, 30))\n",
    "\n",
    "print(f"👤 Detected faces count: {len(faces)}")\n",
    "for idx, (x, y, w, h) in enumerate(faces):\n",
    "    print(f\"Face {idx + 1}: Bounding Box coordinates (x={x}, y={y}, w={w}, h={h})\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## 6. 🎨 Draw Bounding Boxes and Crop Faces\n",
    "\n",
    "Let's write a quick loop to box the detected coordinates on our image, crop the individual face regions, and show them."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "# Draw boxes\n",
    "img_boxed = img.copy()\n",
    "draw = ImageDraw.Draw(img_boxed)\n",
    "\n",
    "for (x, y, w, h) in faces:\n",
    "    draw.rectangle([x, y, x + w, y + h], outline=\"#38ef7d\", width=4)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show annotated image\n",
    "plt.figure(figsize=(8, 8))\n",
    "plt.imshow(img_boxed)\n",
    "plt.axis('off')\n",
    "plt.title(\"Annotated Detection Image\")\n",
    "plt.show()\n",
    "\n",
    "# Show individual face crops\n",
    "fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, len(faces), figsize=(12, 3))\n",
    "if len(faces) == 1:\n",
    "    axes = [axes]\n",
    "    \n",
    "for idx, (x, y, w, h) in enumerate(faces):\n",
    "    crop = img.crop((x, y, x + w, y + h))\n",
    "    axes[idx].imshow(crop)\n",
    "    axes[idx].axis('off')\n",
    "    axes[idx].set_title(f\"Face {idx+1}\")\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.show()"
   ]
  }
 ],
